  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nMidnight radar and satellite imagery depicted several clusters\nof sub-severe thunderstorms across eastern Colorado, making \nslow progress eastward to the Kansas border. Mesoanalysis \nindicates mixed layer CAPE of 1-2k J/kg, and bulk shear of about\n30 kts across the western zones, which should be sufficient to \nallow at least some of this activity to enter the western zones \novernight. 00z ARW supports this notion, but keeps most of the \nshowers and storms west of US 83. NBM seemed too wet, too far \neast and trimmed the pop grids down through sunrise Thursday. \nSimilar to previous nights, uncertainty remains high regarding \nnocturnal thunderstorm coverage overnight in this summer NWly \nmidlevel flow pattern. Some models are aggressive with storm \ncoverage, some models are dry. Given radar/satellite trends in \nColorado, and some favorable parameters in western zones, split \nthe difference. A healthy MCS is possible west of US 83 by 4 am \nshould the 00z ARW and other CAMs come to fruition. \n\nThe strong midlevel high will remain well to the southwest of \nSW KS Thursday, over Arizona and New Mexico. This will help keep\ntemperatures near late August normals Thursday afternoon, in the\nupper 80s and lower 90s. Along with light winds, poor mixing and\nlack of downslope, Thursday will represent a small break from\nthe excessive heat. The forecasted high of 90 at DDC would be\nthe coolest max temperature since August 5th. \n\n00z ARW suggests another mesoscale convective system (MCS) will\ndevelop across Nebraska by 7 pm Thursday, then sink due\nsouthward into central Kansas, east of US 283, Thursday night\nand early Friday. The ARW solution has some support from 00z\nNAM, but again model agreement is mediocre at best, with various\nweak, hard to time vorticity maxima rotating through the\nnortheast periphery of the ridge. The ARW solution would require\nhigh pops for the eastern zones Thursday night, but the NBM,\nincorporating those drier solutions, only included minimal\nslight chance pops across northeast zones Thursday night. The\nuncertainty inherent with summer nocturnal thunderstorm\nforecasting continues. \n\nA strong heating trend is expected Friday through the weekend.\nRising heights and increased south winds and improved mixing\nwill send temperatures well into the 90s Friday afternoon. \nA dry forecast Friday is appropriate, as subsidence increases\nfrom the approaching ridge axis. Heat is expected to peak over\nthe weekend, with afternoon temperatures of 100-105 common both\nSaturday and Sunday afternoons. Model guidance places the upper\nhigh over Texas by Saturday, which will spread SWly midlevel\nflow across the plains, and the return of lee troughing. As\nsuch, the Kansas winds will return Saturday, with south winds\naveraging 20-30 mph with gusts near 40 mph. Air is expected to\nremain sufficiently dry through the weekend, with dewpoints in\nthe upper 50s and lower 60s, such that a heat advisory will not\nbe required for most zones. As usual, the exception may be \nBarber county and the southeast zones where the heat index will \nbe near 105 for a few hours Sunday afternoon. Saturday's \nforecast is dry, but scattered thunderstorms will favor the \nnorthwest zones Sunday afternoon, as more favorable SWly \nmidlevel flow overspreads the plains.",
